@startuml 05-sequence-instructor
' =====================================================================
' Instructor configuration and review
' Traces to: FR-1..FR-11, NFR-9, NFR-10, DR-1..DR-3
' =====================================================================

title Instructor Configuration and Review

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam maxMessageSize 200

actor Instructor as instr
participant "Web\nFrontend" as fe #DCEFD9
participant "Web Backend\n/ REST API" as be #DCEFD9
participant "Repository\nLayer" as repo #DCEFD9
database "Grading DB" as db #DCEFD9

== Configure an assignment and its grading mode [FR-1, FR-6] ==

instr -> fe : create assignment,\nchoose IMMEDIATE or NIGHTLY
fe -> be : POST /assignments\n{platform_assignment_id, name,\ngrading_mode}
be -> repo : create_assignment()
repo -> db : INSERT assignment
be --> fe : 201 {assignment_id}

note right of instr #FFF8DC
  Two separate settings, easily confused:

  **grading_mode** on the ASSIGNMENT says WHEN.
  IMMEDIATE grades during the submission run,
  NIGHTLY defers to a triggered rerun. One
  choice for the whole assignment, so a
  submission is wholly one or the other.

  **feedback_mode** on each TARGET says WHETHER.
  NEVER, ALWAYS, or AUTOGRADER.

  Both live in the database, so changing either
  needs no re-upload of the autograder zip.

  Guidance for the instructor guide: IMMEDIATE
  adds LLM latency inside the autograder
  timeout, and submissions spike before a
  deadline. Large courses should prefer NIGHTLY.
end note

== Define grading targets [FR-2, FR-3, FR-7] ==

loop for each method or class to grade
  instr -> fe : add target\n(identifier, kind, max_score,\nfeedback_mode)
  fe -> be : POST /assignments/{id}/targets
  be -> repo : create_target()
  repo -> db : INSERT grading_target

  instr -> fe : designate relevant files (globs)
  fe -> be : POST /targets/{id}/file-patterns
  be -> repo : set_file_patterns()
  repo -> db : INSERT target_file_pattern
end

note right of instr #FFF8DC
  feedback_mode is the instructor's control over
  token spend:
    AUTOGRADER (default) -- her test suite calls
      request_ai_feedback for the targets it
      wants graded, so correct code costs
      nothing unless she says otherwise.
    ALWAYS -- every submission, for style or
      design feedback no test can detect.
    NEVER -- targets graded purely by tests.

  **The target identifiers registered here are a
  contract with her test suite.** She passes
  these exact strings to record_outcome and
  request_ai_feedback. Rename a target in the
  web app and her suite stops matching, so the
  UI should make renaming visibly consequential
  and the API should reject unknown names
  loudly. See OQ-12.
end note

== Attach rubric and feedback guidance [FR-4, FR-5, FR-13] ==

instr -> fe : write criteria +\nfeedback guidance
fe -> be : PUT /targets/{id}/rubric
be -> repo : upsert_rubric()
repo -> db : INSERT rubric\n(version = n+1, is_current = true)
repo -> db : UPDATE prior rubric\nSET is_current = false

note right of repo #FFF8DC
  Rubrics are versioned, not overwritten.
  feedback rows reference rubric_id, so feedback
  generated last week stays explainable after an
  edit this week.
end note

== Review results [FR-8, FR-9, FR-10, NFR-10] ==

instr -> fe : open results dashboard
fe -> be : GET /assignments/{id}/results\n?filter=has_deduction
be -> repo : list_results()
repo -> db : SELECT joining submission,\ntest_outcome, grading_task, feedback
db --> repo : rows
be --> fe : 200 {results}
fe --> instr : submissions needing attention

instr -> fe : drill into one submission
fe -> be : GET /submissions/{id}
be -> repo : get_submission_detail()
repo -> db : SELECT outcomes, all feedback rows\n(every provider), comparison,\nai_needed, ai_needed_reason, ai_status
db --> repo : detail
be --> fe : 200 {detail}
fe --> instr : side-by-side provider feedback,\nconfidence, and AI status\n[FR-27, NFR-12]

note right of fe #FFF8DC
  Surfacing ai_needed_reason alongside ai_status
  matters: it is how the instructor tells
  "correct code, nothing to say" from "feedback
  is late" from "this target gave up". Without
  the reason, all three render as a blank cell.
end note

== Rate feedback quality [FR-11] ==

instr -> fe : rate feedback (1-5 + comment)
fe -> be : POST /feedback/{id}/review
be -> repo : create_review()
repo -> db : INSERT feedback_review

note over instr, db #FFF8DC
  **OQ-3, open:** can the instructor EDIT
  generated feedback before the student sees it?

  This is now mode-dependent, which makes it
  harder than before. In nightly mode an
  approval gate is possible between PENDING and
  the rerun. In immediate mode there is no
  window: feedback reaches the student in the
  same autograder run that generates it.

  If review-before-release is required, it is an
  argument against offering immediate mode at
  all. Decide before Student B builds the
  dashboard.
end note

@enduml
